1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,005
Lesson 4.1.

2
00:00:02,385 --> 00:00:05,347
Energy-based models and graphical models.

3
00:00:06,450 --> 00:00:09,982
In unit one we saw that a
system at equilibrium visits

4
00:00:09,982 --> 00:00:13,318
each state with probability
proportional to e^(−E).

5
00:00:13,698 --> 00:00:16,328
If we read it the other
way around, any energy

6
00:00:16,328 --> 00:00:18,844
function defines a
probability distribution.

7
00:00:19,224 --> 00:00:21,149
That is an energy-based model.

8
00:00:21,529 --> 00:00:24,684
And it is exactly the
program a thermodynamic chip

9
00:00:24,684 --> 00:00:27,903
runs: we give it an energy,
and it returns samples.

10
00:00:29,017 --> 00:00:30,866
Think of the energy as a landscape.

11
00:00:31,246 --> 00:00:35,486
Valleys are likely configurations
and mountains, unlikely ones.

12
00:00:35,866 --> 00:00:38,319
Learning a model is
sculpting the landscape

13
00:00:38,319 --> 00:00:40,431
so that the valleys fall on the data.

14
00:00:40,811 --> 00:00:43,575
Parameters set the shape
of the landscape, and

15
00:00:43,575 --> 00:00:47,181
temperature decides how much
we explore outside the valleys.

16
00:00:48,283 --> 00:00:53,949
A useful energy is written as a sum of
local terms, each over a few variables.

17
00:00:54,329 --> 00:00:59,134
Then the probability factorizes into
a product of factors, one per term.

18
00:00:59,514 --> 00:01:02,389
We draw one node per
variable and connect those

19
00:01:02,389 --> 00:01:05,264
that share a term: that
is the graphical model.

20
00:01:05,644 --> 00:01:09,687
The key consequence is the Markov
blanket: the distribution of a

21
00:01:09,687 --> 00:01:13,603
variable, given all the others,
depends only on its neighbors.

22
00:01:14,717 --> 00:01:18,844
The factor graph makes the
terms explicit: it is bipartite,

23
00:01:18,844 --> 00:01:22,412
with circles for variables
and squares for factors.

24
00:01:22,792 --> 00:01:25,850
A two-variable factor is an ordinary edge.

25
00:01:26,230 --> 00:01:30,347
A factor of three or more
variables, like s₁ s₂

26
00:01:30,347 --> 00:01:34,378
s₃, is a hyperedge: the
model is a hypergraph.

27
00:01:34,758 --> 00:01:38,662
Current hardware implements
mostly pairwise interactions, so

28
00:01:38,662 --> 00:01:42,437
higher-order terms are rewritten
with auxiliary variables.

29
00:01:43,550 --> 00:01:47,943
Let's see it in a chain
of three spins with

30
00:01:47,943 --> 00:01:52,337
energy E = −s₁s₂ − s₂s₃
at temperature one.

31
00:01:52,717 --> 00:01:58,454
The ends do not interact,
but they are correlated: the

32
00:01:58,454 --> 00:02:04,085
correlation between s₁ and
s₃ is tanh² 1, about 0.58.

33
00:02:04,465 --> 00:02:08,745
If we fix the middle spin, the
correlation vanishes exactly.

34
00:02:09,125 --> 00:02:13,570
Information travels through the graph,
and knowing the neighbor cuts the path.

35
00:02:14,683 --> 00:02:17,679
With only eight configurations
we can compute everything.

36
00:02:18,059 --> 00:02:21,237
The partition function is 19.05.

37
00:02:21,617 --> 00:02:25,935
The two aligned configurations
have probability 0.39 each,

38
00:02:25,935 --> 00:02:29,806
and together add up to
almost seventy-eight percent.

39
00:02:30,186 --> 00:02:33,162
Those with the middle spin against both

40
00:02:33,162 --> 00:02:36,976
neighbors are the least
likely, under one percent.

41
00:02:37,356 --> 00:02:41,508
For a hundred spins we could no longer
enumerate: we will have to sample.

42
00:02:42,617 --> 00:02:45,385
In practice, a program for a thermodynamic

43
00:02:45,385 --> 00:02:48,088
chip is a factor graph of bounded degree.

44
00:02:48,468 --> 00:02:53,496
Libraries like THRML work
directly with factor graphs.

45
00:02:53,876 --> 00:02:57,872
And in machine learning,
Boltzmann machines and the

46
00:02:57,872 --> 00:03:02,104
denoising models of unit
nine are energy-based models.

47
00:03:03,217 --> 00:03:07,473
Common mistakes: believing two variables
without an edge are independent.

48
00:03:07,853 --> 00:03:11,052
Confusing the interaction graph
with the correlation graph.

49
00:03:11,432 --> 00:03:15,303
Thinking the partition function is
easy because each factor is local.

50
00:03:15,683 --> 00:03:18,334
And forgetting that
pairwise hardware needs

51
00:03:18,334 --> 00:03:20,985
auxiliary variables
for higher-order terms.

52
00:03:22,117 --> 00:03:27,456
In summary: an energy-based
model defines p ∝ e^(−E).

53
00:03:27,836 --> 00:03:30,623
If the energy is a sum of local terms, the

54
00:03:30,623 --> 00:03:34,207
probability factorizes and
is drawn as a factor graph.

55
00:03:34,587 --> 00:03:37,653
Each variable depends only
on its Markov blanket, and

56
00:03:37,653 --> 00:03:40,430
conditional independence
is read from the graph.

57
00:03:41,550 --> 00:03:46,022
To practice: draw a factor
graph with a three-body term.

58
00:03:46,402 --> 00:03:49,257
Find the Markov blanket
in a square lattice.

59
00:03:49,637 --> 00:03:52,588
Prove conditional
independence in the chain.

60
00:03:52,968 --> 00:03:56,981
And count the terms in the partition
function of a ten-by-ten lattice.
